# Datastore sizing defaults — the SINGLE source of truth for every install artifact.
#
# Why a shared file instead of the number typed into each template: the same
# size has to appear in artifacts written in five different languages (bash,
# compose YAML, CloudFormation YAML, Railway JSON, Helm YAML). Nothing but a
# gate keeps hand-copied constants together, so this file is the value and
# ci/check-storage-defaults.sh asserts every artifact still matches it. Same
# shape as ci/critical-gateway-env.txt + ci/check-service-env-coverage.sh, which
# gate env-var coverage for the same reason.
#
# WHERE THE NUMBER COMES FROM
#
# The gateway keeps 90 days of trace content by default
# (TRACE_RETENTION_DEFAULT_DAYS in @anyray/observability; the operator shortens
# the window with ANYRAY_SPEND_RETENTION_DAYS, which prunes BOTH spend rows and
# traces). So every datastore must survive 90 days of accumulation before the
# first prune reclaims anything — a volume that fills stops Postgres, and takes
# the spend and trace stores down with it.
#
# Content is what grows. Measured on Anyray's own deployment (2026-07-30):
# encrypted trace content added ~1 GB/day of disk under heavy whole-team + CI
# agent usage (anyray_observations 12 GB + anyray_traces 11 GB, nearly all
# TOAST; anyray_spend was 118 MB against the same traffic). Managed client
# deployments over the same period sat near 1 GB total. That is a ~90x spread,
# so these defaults target the typical deployment and the docs carry the
# measurement + expansion path for the heavy end.
#
# 2026-09-14: RAISED 50 -> 150. A deployment storing raw content reached 9.3 GB
# in 16 days, about 0.5 GB/day, which projects to ~45 GB at 90 days. That fits
# inside 50 GB only on paper: it leaves ~10% headroom on a volume that cannot be
# grown in place on every platform, and the measured heavy case in the paragraph
# above is ~1 GB/day, which is 90 GB and does NOT fit at all. Both numbers were
# already in this file; the default was sized for the typical case and then used
# everywhere.
#
# The trade is deliberate and cheap. Block storage runs about $0.15/GB-month, so
# 150 GB is roughly $22/month against the thousands of dollars a month of
# inference spend a deployment of that size saves. Over-provisioning wastes a
# rounding error; under-provisioning fills the volume, and a full volume stops
# Postgres and takes the spend store, the trace store AND client-key
# verification down with it. Never trade a customer's data or uptime for a
# number this small. The alternative -- shortening the retention window we
# promise -- was tried in ANY-402 and rejected for exactly that reason.
#
# Still free headroom on OCI, whose block volumes bill a 50 GB minimum.
ANYRAY_DB_STORAGE_GB=150

# RDS-only: upper bound for storage autoscaling. The database grows for its
# first three months on a new install, so autoscaling is what keeps a heavy
# deployment off a storage-full stall without provisioning for the worst case
# on day one.
#
# This one matters more than the allocation, and costs nothing until used: RDS
# grows on demand, so the ceiling is a permission to grow rather than a purchase.
# Raised 100 -> 400 to cover the ~1 GB/day heavy case with room, since hitting
# the ceiling stalls writes exactly like a full disk.
ANYRAY_DB_MAX_STORAGE_GB=400

# Single-VM installs: the persistent data disk carries Docker's data-root, so
# it holds the Postgres volume AND the gateway/optimizer /data volumes. Sized
# above ANYRAY_DB_STORAGE_GB because it carries the images and both /data
# volumes on top of Postgres.
ANYRAY_VM_DATA_DISK_GB=200

# DELIBERATE CARVE-OUT, do not "fix" this to match the value above.
#
# helm/values.yaml keeps postgres.storage at 10Gi. The bundled Postgres is a
# StatefulSet and its size lands in a volumeClaimTemplate, which Kubernetes
# makes IMMUTABLE after the first install. Raising the CHART default would make
# the next `helm upgrade` fail for every existing install that never pinned the
# value, with an opaque "updates to statefulset spec for fields other than
# 'replicas' are forbidden" error, and self-hosted clients upgrade on their own
# schedule so they would hit it unattended.
#
# New installs get the real size a different way: setup.sh --k8s and the
# GKE/AKS deploy scripts write postgres.storage into the generated values file,
# which only ever affects a fresh install. check-storage-defaults.sh asserts
# BOTH halves — that the generated values carry ANYRAY_DB_STORAGE_GB, and that
# the chart default is still this value.
ANYRAY_HELM_CHART_DEFAULT_STORAGE=10Gi
